jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.27-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [DOC] Document that pure-portal must be the array's VIRTUAL management IP
    (vir0), not the address of an individual controller (ct0.eth0 /
    ct1.eth0). A FlashArray is assigned three management addresses at setup:
    one per controller, plus a virtual IP bound to whichever controller
    currently holds the management primary role. Pointed at a controller's own
    address, the plugin loses the REST API the moment that controller fails
    over: activate_storage() cannot reach the array and the storage goes
    inactive after three consecutive failed polls. Running guests are
    unaffected - status() failing does not touch any device, so multipath maps
    stay mapped and I/O continues on the data path - but every operation
    needing the array's API stops, along with the capacity figures.
    This is the same requirement Pure places on its other integrations: the
    OpenStack Cinder driver documentation states that "the Management VIP
    address is required to properly configure the FlashArray driver", and the
    FlashArray vSphere Plugin refuses to install with a "No virtual IP
    configured" error.
    Added to both READMEs (with the remove-and-re-add procedure, since
    pure-portal is a fixed property that cannot be changed with `pvesm set`),
    to the documentation site, and to the pure-portal property description
    itself so it appears in `pvesm set --help` and the API schema.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 11:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.26-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [MEDIUM] Cut the steady-state REST load on the array's management gateway
    roughly in half. Counting the calls a single pvestatd poll makes per node
    per storage found four avoidable ones:
    - get_managed_capacity() fetched the pod object, then called
      pod_get_quota_limit() which fetched the SAME pod object again. The
      already-fetched object is now passed in.
    - _detect_api_version() ran for every new client, costing at least one
      unauthenticated GET /api/api_version and up to nine more probes on
      failure. The plugin's client cache expires after 300s and the
      background reaper forks and therefore always builds a fresh client, so
      this repeated constantly. The detected version is now cached per array
      for the life of the process. A fallback guess is deliberately not
      cached.
    - A forked reaper pass logged in again. A Pure x-auth-token is a bearer
      token, so a new client can be seeded with an existing session; what
      must not be shared across a fork is the keep-alive socket, and each
      client still builds its own. A stale token costs one 401, after which
      the existing retry path re-logs in.
    - activate_storage() asked for the iSCSI port list and re-verified the
      host object on every poll, though neither changes in normal operation.
      Both are now cached per process for API_CACHE_TTL (300s). The host
      entry is recorded only after the check actually succeeds, so a
      transient failure cannot silence the check for a whole TTL.
    Measured over three consecutive polls in a harness with a counting API
    stub: 15 calls before, 8 after.
  * [MEDIUM] Never retry a rename. volume_rename() and snapshot_rename() are
    PATCH requests, and _request() only excluded POST from its 5xx retry -
    while LWP reports a read timeout as a synthetic 500. If the array
    performed the rename and the response was lost, the retry addressed a name
    that no longer existed, came back 404, and the caller concluded the rename
    had failed. In volume_delete()'s tombstone path that meant destroying
    under the ORIGINAL name (404), reporting the delete as failed, and leaving
    a renamed-but-alive volume on the array; the operator's retry then found
    nothing under the original name and reported success, stranding the volume
    permanently. Both renames now use a new no_retry option and, on failure,
    verify against the array whether the rename actually took effect before
    believing the error.
  * [MEDIUM] A 'pure-pod' naming a pod that does not exist is now a hard
    error. The capacity lookup fell back to the array total, so a typo made
    the storage report the whole array as free while every volume create
    failed because the pod was missing.
  * Documented that two storages sharing one pod double-count capacity in
    Proxmox VE (volume names stay isolated; only the figures overlap).

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 18:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.25-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [MEDIUM] Stop storing the Pure API token and password in
    /etc/pve/storage.cfg. PVE 9 lets a plugin declare which of its properties
    are sensitive, via plugindata()->{'sensitive-properties'}; the storage
    config API then pulls those out of the request before the config is
    written and hands them to on_add_hook / on_update_hook instead. We never
    declared it, so the list fell back to PVE's hardcoded
    "encryption-key keyring master-pubkey password", which covers neither of
    ours. The token therefore sat in storage.cfg in cleartext and
    `GET /storage/<id>` returned it to anyone holding Datastore.Allocate on
    that storage - which is not root. A Pure API token is typically
    array-wide, so that is full control of the FlashArray rather than of one
    storage. Credentials now live in /etc/pve/priv/storage/<id>.pure-token
    and .pure-pw (mode 0600, in a directory pmxcfs keeps root-only), the same
    place PBSPlugin and CIFSPlugin keep theirs.
  * Existing installations keep working with no action required:
    _resolve_credentials() prefers the out-of-config secret and falls back to
    the value in storage.cfg, and a PVE storage update is a merge, so an
    unrelated `pvesm set` can never drop the in-config token.
  * To complete the migration and remove the cleartext copy, run once per
    storage: `pvesm set <id> --pure-api-token <token>`. That writes the
    secret file AND removes the old line from storage.cfg in a single
    command. Do NOT use `--delete pure-api-token` for this: PVE reports a
    deletion to the plugin as an explicit removal, so it would delete the
    secret file as well and leave the storage with no credentials.
  * [MEDIUM] Honour the volume name PVE asks for. PVE requests a specific
    name in four places - vm-<vmid>-state-<snap> (RAM snapshot),
    vm-<vmid>-cloudinit, vm-<vmid>-fleece-<n> (backup fleecing) and whatever
    the operator types to `pvesm alloc`. Anything not recognised fell through
    to the regular-disk branch, which ignored the requested name and
    allocated vm-<vmid>-disk-<N> instead. Backup fleecing images therefore
    came back looking like ordinary VM disks and consumed a disk id in the
    guest's numbering. Fleecing is now a first-class volume type
    (pve-<storage>-<vmid>-fleece<n> on the array, excluded from disk-id
    allocation), and an unrecognised explicit name is refused with a message
    naming the four supported forms rather than silently substituted -
    LVMPlugin and ZFSPoolPlugin accept any vm-<vmid>-<...> for the same
    reason.
  * _get_api() now requires the storeid, since that is how the credentials
    are located. All 21 call sites pass it, and tools/audit-invariants.pl
    enforces this statically so a future call site cannot omit it.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 16:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.24-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [CRITICAL] Report linked clones as "base-X-disk-N/vm-Y-disk-M" from
    list_images(), matching what clone_image() returns and what the guest
    config stores. Reporting the bare "vm-Y-disk-M" made
    PVE::QemuServer::update_disk_config (which `qm rescan` drives) see the
    volume as unreferenced, because it compares the config's volid against
    the volid the plugin reports. PVE then called add_unused_volume(), so the
    guest gained an "unusedN" entry pointing at the SAME Pure volume its
    scsi0 was running on - and removing that "unused disk" in the GUI
    destroys the live disk. The base volume is derived from Pure's `source`
    field, accepting only a ".pve-base" snapshot source so that full clones
    and clones from user snapshots keep their bare name.
    RBDPlugin::list_images does the equivalent from the rbd parent snapshot.
  * [HIGH] Release the snapshot-access clone that container backups leave
    behind. PVE::VZDump::LXC calls activate_volumes($cfg, $volids, 'vzdump'),
    which creates a clone on the array through our path(), then mounts,
    rsyncs, umounts and deletes the snapshot - and never calls
    deactivate_volume at all, so _cleanup_temp_snap_clone() was never
    invoked. That leaked one Pure volume, one host connection and one local
    multipath device per container mountpoint per backup. The one-hour orphan
    reaper was the only backstop, and it never ran at all on an API 2.x array
    before 1.1.22, so sites with scheduled container backups have been
    accumulating these since installation. Cleanup is now triggered from
    volume_snapshot_delete(), which PVE does reliably call, with the same
    ownership, age and in-use gates as the background reaper.
  * [HIGH] Propagate capacity changes to the local device. The refresh in
    volume_resize() was gated on $running, so resizing a stopped VM's disk
    left the local multipath map at the old size - start the guest and qemu
    presents the old capacity, silently. The same applied to resizing on one
    node and starting the guest on another, which no gating could have
    covered. The refresh now runs unconditionally (a no-op without a local
    device) and activate_volume() reconciles the array size it already
    fetched against blockdev --getsize64, rescanning only on a mismatch. The
    whole refresh is best-effort: the array-side resize has already
    succeeded, so a local failure must not be reported as a failed resize.
  * [MEDIUM] Match $vollist exactly in list_images(). $vollist holds complete
    volids and the base plugin compares them exactly; a prefix match returned
    "vm-10-disk-10" and "vm-10-disk-11" when asked for "vm-10-disk-1".

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 14:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.23-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [CRITICAL] Make the in-use safety guard fail CLOSED. is_device_in_use()
    collapsed "I could not determine the answer" into "not in use": every
    internal failure (a /proc/mounts read that timed out, a `fuser` call
    killed by its own 5s watchdog, a device path that would not resolve)
    fell through to `return 0`. For a raw Pure LUN attached to a running VM
    there is no mount and no real holder - the kpartx partitions are
    deliberately ignored - so `fuser` is the ONLY positive signal that a
    guest has the device open. A timed-out `fuser` therefore turned "a VM is
    using this disk" into "nothing is using this disk", precisely when the
    node was unhealthy enough for that watchdog to fire, and free_image()
    would go on to disconnect and destroy the volume. New
    device_usage_state() returns in-use / idle / unknown with a reason;
    is_device_in_use() now treats unknown as in use.
  * [CRITICAL] A failed WWID lookup no longer disables the safety check.
    free_image(), volume_snapshot_rollback() and create_base() each did
    `my $wwid = eval { ... }; if ($wwid) { ...check... }` and then destroyed
    or overwrote regardless, so one transient REST error ran the operation
    unprotected. Rollback was the worst case: volume_overwrite() replaces the
    volume's contents outright and, unlike a destroy, has no eradication-delay
    recovery window. Both now refuse with an actionable message after a
    bounded retry.
  * [HIGH] No automated path performs an unrecoverable eradication any more.
    The orphaned temp-clone reaper and the per-session temp-clone cleanup
    called volume_delete() without skip_eradicate, i.e. DELETE /volumes -
    permanent, with no recovery window, from a background reaper. Both now
    soft-destroy. Note this reaper only began running at all in 1.1.22: its
    age check could never be true on an API 2.x array before the millisecond
    timestamp fix.
  * [HIGH] The temp-clone reaper now re-matches candidates locally against
    the exact name path() generates
    (pve-<storage>-...-temp-snap-access-<unix-ts>-<pid>) instead of acting on
    whatever the loose array-side glob returned, and requires two independent
    age sources to agree - the array's created timestamp and the unix
    timestamp baked into the name - before deleting.
  * [HIGH] alloc_image() no longer destroys a pre-existing state/cloudinit
    volume without checking whether it is in use. This was the only
    destructive path in the plugin with no in-use check at all; a
    vm-<id>-state-<snap> volume holding a live suspended guest's RAM image
    would be discarded with only a warn().
  * [HIGH] Fix two mirrored volume_list() calls that passed named arguments
    to a positional-only function, so the pattern bound to the literal string
    "pattern" and the array was asked for a volume with that exact name.
    _cleanup_vm_config_volumes() therefore never deleted anything, and
    free_image()'s "is this the VM's last disk?" test was always true. The two
    bugs cancelled out into "config backup volumes leak". Fixing only one side
    would have started destroying config backups on the FIRST disk deletion of
    a multi-disk VM while snapshots of the remaining disks still referenced
    them, so both are fixed together, and free_image() now skips the cleanup
    rather than guessing when the disk list cannot be retrieved.
  * [MEDIUM] Device lookup failure during a destructive operation is now an
    error rather than being read as "there is no local device".
  * [LOW] postinst adds `fuser` to its required-binary check; it is the
    load-bearing check for a running guest and psmisc is already a hard
    dependency.

  * [HIGH] Stop issuing a Fibre Channel LIP on every rescan. A Loop
    Initialization Primitive is a link reset that forces every device behind
    that HBA port to re-login, including LUNs belonging to other storage on
    the same HBA. It is not needed to discover a newly mapped LUN - the SCSI
    host scan does that via REPORT_LUNS on the existing session. It was being
    issued unconditionally from tight loops: path()'s retry loop (every 2s),
    alloc_image()'s wait loop (every 3s), wait_for_multipath_device()'s FC
    callback (every round) and, before 1.1.22, activate_storage() on every
    pvestatd poll. LIP is now opt-in via rescan_fc_hosts(lip => 1) and is not
    used anywhere by default.
  * [HIGH] FC.pm now reads sysfs through sysfs_read_with_timeout instead of a
    bare open(). A read of /sys/class/fc_host/*/port_state blocks
    indefinitely on a wedged HBA, and get_fc_targets() runs from
    activate_storage() on the pvestatd path.
  * [HIGH] pve-pure-config-get no longer uses bare system() for mount and
    umount. A mount against a multipath device whose paths are gone enters
    uninterruptible sleep and never returns - and this tool only ever runs
    when storage is already in trouble. All external commands now go through
    PVE::Tools::run_command with an explicit timeout.
  * [HIGH] pve-pure-config-get picks the NEWEST destroyed generation when a
    disk has been deleted and recreated several times. Sorting tombstone
    names lexically selected the OLDEST generation, and the newer ones then
    hit a rename-back conflict that aborted the whole restore. Selection now
    uses the unix timestamp embedded in the tombstone suffix, and the tool
    prints which generation it chose and what else was available.
  * [HIGH] The orphaned temp-clone reaper now skips clones still connected to
    another node. A temp clone is connected only to the node that created it,
    but every node runs the reaper: a snapshot-source operation running longer
    than an hour on node A (a qemu-img convert of a large disk routinely is)
    had its device disconnected and destroyed by node B, which has no local
    device and therefore sailed past the in-use check that protects node A.
  * [MEDIUM] remove_scsi_device() verifies the device still carries the
    expected WWID before deleting it. free_image() captures the multipath
    slave list before the array-side disconnect and deletes those slaves
    afterwards; the kernel reuses /dev/sdX names, so a concurrent rescan could
    hand the same name to an unrelated LUN whose path would then be removed.
  * [MEDIUM] volume_get_connections() distinguishes "no connections" from "the
    query failed". Returning an empty list for both made free_image() skip the
    disconnect and destroy anyway, leaving orphaned host connections that
    surface as ghost LUNs on other nodes. free_image() now refuses to delete
    when connections cannot be listed.
  * [MEDIUM] pve-pure-config-get uses PVE::INotify::nodename() instead of
    `hostname -s`, so it connects volumes to the same Pure host object the
    plugin registered, and wraps device cleanup so a refusal cannot abort a
    restore that has already written the config.
  * [MEDIUM] Remaining unbounded waits removed: the intermediate child in
    status() and the success path of sysfs_read_with_timeout() now reap with
    WNOHANG on a bounded poll.
  * [LOW] _run_cmd and sysfs_read_with_timeout save and restore any alarm the
    caller had armed, instead of clearing it with a bare alarm(0).
  * [LOW] pve-pure-config-get reports its real version; dead code removed from
    FC.pm.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 10:30:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.22-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [CRITICAL] Stop performing a full SAN rescan on every pvestatd poll.
    Proxmox VE calls activate_storage() from storage_info() on every poll
    (~10s), sequentially for every storage. activate_storage
    unconditionally ran an iSCSI session rescan, a SCSI host scan, a
    host-wide "multipathd reconfigure" and "udevadm trigger
    --subsystem-match=block" plus "udevadm settle" on each of those calls,
    i.e. a full multipath rebuild and a re-trigger of every block device on
    the node six times a minute. Besides the standing cost, this competed
    directly with device discovery: a VM start or backup waiting for a new
    LUN was racing a reconfigure that repeatedly tore down and rebuilt the
    map table. Rescans now run immediately when this node logs in to a new
    iSCSI portal, and otherwise at most once per new option
    pure-rescan-interval (default 300s, 0 restores the old behaviour).
    Discovery of new LUNs is unaffected: activate_volume(), path() and
    alloc_image() each perform their own targeted rescan and wait.
  * [HIGH] Fix a potential unkillable hang inside the command-timeout
    handler in Multipath.pm and ISCSI.pm. On timeout the handler ran
    kill('TERM') followed by a blocking waitpid(). A child in
    uninterruptible sleep - the exact case these timeouts exist for -
    cannot be killed, so the blocking waitpid never returned and the alarm
    that would have broken us out had already been cleared. Now escalates
    TERM to KILL and reaps only with WNOHANG on a bounded poll.
  * [HIGH] Rework the device-discovery wait loop (issue #13).
    wait_for_multipath_device() probed for the device only at the END of a
    body that could consume the entire timeout budget (per-session iSCSI
    rescan, per-host SCSI scan, multipathd reconfigure, udev trigger and
    settle), so on a fabric with slow paths the caller got a single look at
    the device, taken right after a host-wide reconfigure. The loop now
    probes before doing any work, probes again after each step, polls
    cheaply between steps, is deadline-aware at every stage, and reaches
    for a host-wide reconfigure only from the second round and at most once
    per 30s process-wide.
  * [HIGH] Do not run orphaned temp-clone cleanup in activate_storage. It
    disconnects and destroys volumes on the array - mutating, potentially
    slow work that does not belong on a path polled every ~10s with the
    short-timeout health client. status() already forks it into the
    background reaper under a lock with the resilient client.
  * [HIGH] Report far more diagnostic detail when a device does not appear.
    The error now includes what multipathd currently sees (map count, Pure
    map count, whether a map exists for the WWID, whether its /dev/mapper
    node was created), matching /dev/disk/by-id links, and per-session iSCSI
    state read from sysfs - captured at failure time rather than left for
    the operator to reproduce.
  * [MEDIUM] Fix Pure REST 2.x timestamp handling. REST 2.x returns
    milliseconds since the epoch, REST 1.x an ISO 8601 string; the code
    assumed the opposite. Two consequences: snapshot ctime reported to
    Proxmox VE was a millisecond value read as seconds, so the Web UI
    showed nonsensical snapshot dates; and the orphaned temp-clone reaper's
    "older than one hour" test could never be true on a 2.x array, so
    orphaned temporary snapshot clones were never cleaned up, each holding
    a volume slot, a host connection on every node and a stale multipath
    device.
  * [MEDIUM] Fix host lookup for the connect-to-all-nodes step. API 2.x
    does not accept wildcards in the "names" query parameter, so
    host_list("pve-<cluster>-*") always came back empty and new volumes
    were pre-connected to the local node only. Wildcards now go through the
    filter parameter, as volume_list already did.
  * [MEDIUM] Pod capacity reporting (issue #10). "used" is now clamped to
    the pod quota so Proxmox VE is never handed used > total, and a pod at
    or over its quota logs an explanation of what that does and does not
    mean, including the raw pod space figures the array returned. New
    option pure-pod-usage-metric (provisioned, default / virtual /
    physical) selects which figure is reported.
  * [MEDIUM] Remove the per-volume WWID lookup from deactivate_storage; the
    WWID is derived from the serial the volume list already returned. The
    old form was an N+1 REST storm against the array, fired exactly when a
    node is shutting down.
  * [MEDIUM] Fix the API client cache key. It keyed on $scfg->{storage},
    which Proxmox VE never populates, so it degraded to the portal address
    alone: two storages on the same array with different API tokens or
    different pure-status-timeout values shared one cached client.
  * [LOW] filesystem_path() now fails with an actionable message instead of
    a bare "storage is required" from inside the naming module. It passed
    $scfg->{storage} as the storage id, which is always undef.
  * [LOW] Remove the argument-less multipath_flush() call in
    deactivate_storage, which could only ever throw (the helper refuses to
    run "multipath -F", which would flush every unused map on the host).
  * [LOW] postinst no longer reports another vendor's multipath settings as
    a Pure hazard. The dangerous-settings check grepped the whole of
    /etc/multipath.conf with no scope awareness, so `no_path_retry queue`
    inside a `device { vendor "NETAPP" }` block - which cannot affect Pure
    devices, and is that vendor's own recommendation - was reported as a
    Pure risk. The check now tracks scope and only warns for settings in
    `defaults` or in a PURE device block; settings scoped to another
    vendor are reported as a note for awareness.
  * [LOW] activate_volume() now tracks the volume WWID, closing a gap where
    a volume activated without path() being called was invisible to the
    cluster residual-device cleanup.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Sun, 26 Jul 2026 18:20:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.21-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [HIGH] Isolate the pvestatd health path from a slow array. New
    option pure-status-timeout (default 5s): activate_storage() and
    the foreground of status() now use a short-timeout, single-attempt
    REST client instead of the resilient data-path client (15s x 2
    retries). PVE polls storages sequentially every ~10s, so a slow or
    degraded array previously backed up the whole pvestatd cycle and
    starved sibling storages on the same node into "inactive". Dropping
    per-call retries on this path costs nothing because the next poll
    is the retry. The data path (alloc/free/clone) and the background
    reaper keep the resilient client.
  * [HIGH] Enable HTTP keep-alive on the REST client. The LWP::UserAgent
    now reuses one TCP+TLS connection across calls instead of opening a
    fresh connection (and TLS handshake) per request, reducing the
    steady-state load the plugin's polling places on the array's
    management gateway.
  * [MEDIUM] Bound the cumulative time of the iSCSI portal discover/
    login loop in activate_storage(). New option pure-activate-deadline
    (default 30s): once the budget is spent and at least one portal is
    logged in, remaining portals are deferred to a later activation.
    The budget never applies while zero paths are up and never
    interrupts an in-progress login, so a slow-but-reachable storage is
    never marked inactive. The iSCSI session list is now snapshotted
    once before the loop instead of being queried per portal.
  * [LOW] Compute the temp-clone WWID locally from the serial already
    present in the volume_list() response (serial_to_wwid) instead of a
    per-volume volume_get_wwid() REST call. _cleanup_orphaned_temp_clones
    runs from the status() background reaper on every poll; this removes
    the per-volume round-trip, matching the orphan reaper.
  * [LOW] postinst now prints a prominent warning after an upgrade that
    the operator must run "systemctl restart pvestatd" on every cluster
    node to activate the new plugin code. The package intentionally uses
    "systemctl reload" (SIGHUP) to avoid the stop-phase hang on D-state
    children, but on many PVE versions reload does not reload Perl
    modules, so pvestatd can keep running stale code. The warning shows
    how to verify the MainPID changed.

