jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.19-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [HIGH] volume_list(), volume_list_destroyed() and snapshot_list()
    now follow the API 2.x continuation_token across pages. Pure
    FlashArray REST 2.x caps each GET on a collection endpoint at a
    server page (default ~1000 items) and signals further pages via
    more_items_remaining + continuation_token. The previous code read
    only the first page, so a storage with more than one page of
    volumes or snapshots silently truncated: list_images() hid disks
    from the Proxmox VE web UI and orphan cleanup never saw the tail
    volumes. New API::_get_v2_collection() helper walks all pages
    (bounded by a 100k-page safety stop). API 1.x is unaffected
    (it returns the full set in one response). host_get_volumes()
    is intentionally left single-page: it backs an emptiness gate
    (logout iSCSI sessions only when no volumes remain), where a
    truncated-but-non-empty page yields the same decision.
  * [MEDIUM] Background orphan cleanup no longer issues one extra
    volume_get_wwid() REST call per volume on every pvestatd poll
    (~10s). _cleanup_orphaned_devices() Phase 1 now derives the WWID
    from the serial already present in the volume_list() response
    (API::serial_to_wwid), falling back to the per-volume lookup only
    when serial is absent. On a storage with hundreds/thousands of
    volumes this removes a per-poll burst of N REST calls that scaled
    with volume count and could hit Pure's API rate limit.
  * [MEDIUM] Background cleanup is now serialised per storeid with a
    non-blocking flock. status() forks a cleanup pass on every
    pvestatd poll; on a large array a single pass can exceed the 10s
    poll interval, so passes would stack into multiple concurrent
    grandchildren all hitting the REST API and local block layer.
    Overlapping polls now skip the work instead of piling on. The
    lock auto-releases on process exit, so it cannot wedge.
  * [HIGH] Orphan reaper no longer reaps a device on a single
    absent observation. Two independent guards were added to
    _cleanup_orphaned_devices(): a grace period (a WWID first seen
    less than 600s ago is never reaped, protecting a just-added LUN
    during the window after map/connect but before qemu opens it,
    when is_device_in_use() legitimately reports idle), and absence
    hysteresis (a WWID must be reported absent from the array for 3
    consecutive cleanup passes before teardown). The tracking-file
    entry schema gained a per-WWID absence counter; older files
    storing a bare timestamp are read transparently. Cross-project
    hardening from a jt-pve-storage-netapp incident where a
    freshly-added, in-use LUN was reaped.
  * [HIGH] Orphan reaper no longer mis-flags a second purestorage
    storage's live device as a stale orphan. Phase 3's warning
    compared host-wide Pure multipath devices against ONE storage's
    tracking; on a host with multiple purestorage storages (multiple
    pods or arrays) a sibling storage's in-use device satisfied
    "not on this array and not tracked here" and prompted the
    operator to run `multipath -f` on a different storage's disk.
    Phase 3 now unions the WWIDs tracked by every other purestorage
    storage on the node and skips them. (Parity with
    jt-pve-storage-netapp v0.2.15.)
  * [MEDIUM] status() now performs outage detection and capacity
    health monitoring. After STATUS_FAIL_THRESHOLD (3) consecutive
    failed polls it logs a single outage ERROR (re-emitted at most
    every 30s while down) and an INFO on recovery; it warns at
    >=90% (WARNING) and >=95% (ERROR) capacity used, rate-limited to
    once per hour. Events are logged via the journal with a
    `pure-storage:` tag for monitoring pickup. (Parity with
    jt-pve-storage-netapp v0.2.10.)
  * [MEDIUM] activate_storage() warns (once per 24h) when every
    reachable iSCSI portal resolves to a single Pure controller
    (CT0/CT1), i.e. this node has no controller-level path
    redundancy. (Parity with jt-pve-storage-netapp v0.2.11.)
  * [LOW] postinst now detects storage-affecting operations in
    flight (qmrestore, vzdump, qm move-disk/clone/migrate,
    pvesm alloc/free/import/export) before reloading PVE services
    and prints a NOTICE with a 5s grace window. The reload is a
    graceful SIGHUP and does not abort the operations. (Parity with
    jt-pve-storage-netapp v0.2.10.)

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 29 May 2026 10:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.18-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [MEDIUM] snapshot_delete() now pre-renames the snapshot to a
    tombstone suffix before destroy (issue #11), mirroring the
    volume-side tombstone fix from v1.1.15. Pure's destroyed-pending
    state reserves a snapshot's suffix for the array's eradication
    delay (default 24h), so without the rename, creating a new
    snapshot with the same name within that window fails with
    "Snapshot 't1' already exists for volume". The pre-rename
    moves the destroyed snapshot under a `<orig-suffix>-pve-tomb-
    <unix-ts>-<pid>` suffix so the original suffix is freed
    immediately. Implementation uses Pure's PATCH /volume-snapshots
    rename API (which takes the new SUFFIX in the body, NOT the
    full name — verified against the FA 2.x OpenAPI spec). Edge
    cases handled: 64-char snapshot-suffix limit overflow falls
    back to direct destroy + warning, already-tombstoned suffixes
    are not re-renamed, rollback rename on destroy failure
    restores the original suffix for a clean retry. API 1.x lacks
    snapshot rename support in the REST surface, so the tombstone
    path is API 2.x only — 1.x destroys go straight through under
    the original name. Reported by @pulipulichen (#11).

  * [LOW] Configurable shorter timeout for the auxiliary
    config-backup volume's multipath device wait (issue #12). The
    plugin creates a 1 MB auxiliary volume on every snapshot to
    archive the VM/CT config (used only by pve-pure-config-get
    for disaster recovery — non-critical). Previously this used
    pure-device-timeout (default 60s), and on degraded multipath
    the snapshot operation would visibly stall the full timeout
    even though the resulting "skipping config backup" warning is
    non-fatal. New storage option `pure-config-backup-timeout`
    (integer 5..60, default 15) sets a separate, shorter wait.
    Warning text expanded to make the non-fatal nature explicit
    and point at the new option. Reported by @pulipulichen (#12).

  * [LOW] postinst now sanity-checks that the required external
    binaries (multipathd, multipath, kpartx, iscsiadm, sg_inq,
    blockdev) are on PATH before proceeding (issue #9). Catches
    the "I installed with dpkg -i and got 'No such file or
    directory' for /sbin/multipathd later" failure mode and
    redirects the operator at `apt --fix-broken install` or
    `apt install ./*.deb`. README install instructions now lead
    with `apt install ./*.deb`, with an explicit warning against
    bare `dpkg -i`. Declared Depends were already correct
    (multipath-tools, open-iscsi, sg3-utils, psmisc); the new
    check just gives a clear error when dpkg is used in a way
    that bypasses Depends enforcement. Reported by @pulipulichen
    (#9).

