jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.16-1) stable; urgency=high

  * [HIGH] pve-pure-config-get restore mode: tombstone-aware recovery.
    v1.1.15 made volume_delete pre-rename volumes to
    "<orig>-pve-tomb-<unix-ts>-<pid>" before destroy. The disaster-
    recovery tool pve-pure-config-get queries Pure's destroyed-
    volumes list when in restore mode (--restore) to find both
    config-backup volumes and the VM's disk volumes, recover them,
    and reconstruct the VM config on this PVE node. After v1.1.15,
    the destroyed volumes the tool finds carry the tombstone suffix.
    Two consequences:
    (a) decode_config_volume_name's greedy snapname capture pulled
        the "-pve-tomb-<ts>-<pid>" trailer into the displayed
        snapname, making the restore picker show ugly nonsense
        instead of just "snapname1".
    (b) MORE SERIOUS: recovered disk volumes lived under their
        tombstone names on Pure (e.g. pve-pure1-100-disk0-pve-tomb-
        1747000000-12345), but the VM config the tool wrote to
        /etc/pve/qemu-server/<vmid>.conf referenced the original
        PVE volname (vm-100-disk-0), which the plugin maps to
        pve-pure1-100-disk0. PVE looked up the disk by its
        original name, did not find it (it was under the tombstone
        name), and the restored VM failed to start.
    Fixed by:
    - Stripping the tombstone suffix before passing volume names to
      decode_config_volume_name for display.
    - Renaming each recovered volume BACK to its original name
      immediately after volume_recover, so the restored VM's disks
      live on Pure under the names PVE expects.
    - Detecting rename-back conflicts (extremely unlikely, but
      possible if the operator already recreated the VM with the
      original name) and aborting the restore with a clear error
      that lists the conflicting tombstone names and offers two
      recovery paths (manual cleanup OR restore to a different
      VMID).
    Also picked up the still-broken inline storeid_to_pure_prefix
    duplication that issue #6 missed in this tool (line 197-198),
    so dotted storage IDs now work end-to-end through the restore
    workflow as well.

  * make test now also syntax-checks bin/pve-pure-config-get, so a
    Perl typo in the tool fails CI / local test rather than being
    discovered only when an operator runs it in a disaster.

