jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.15-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [MEDIUM] Deleted PVE disks no longer hold their Pure volume name
    in destroyed-pending state for the array's eradication delay
    (default 24h). volume_delete now renames the volume to
    "<orig>-pve-tomb-<unix-ts>-<pid>" BEFORE destroying it. The
    original name is freed as soon as the rename succeeds, so the
    user can immediately recreate a same-named disk. The tombstoned
    volume still goes into destroyed-pending (still recoverable
    from Pure UI, still eradicates per Pure's normal schedule),
    just under the suffixed name. Operators can identify these in
    Pure's Destroyed Volumes list by the "-pve-tomb-" marker.
    Reported by @pulipulichen (#8).

  * Edge cases handled in tombstone path:
    - Pod-prefixed volumes (pod::vol) keep the pod prefix on rename;
      63-char length limit checked against the post-"::" portion only.
    - Names that would exceed Pure's 63-char volume-name limit after
      adding the tombstone suffix: skip rename, destroy with original
      name (accept the 24h name reservation rather than risk a
      truncated-name collision), warn so operator can see.
    - Volumes that already carry the "-pve-tomb-" marker (e.g. a
      re-destroy after a previous failed destroy): skip rename to
      avoid recursive double-tombstoning.
    - PID suffix on top of unix timestamp: different PVE nodes
      deleting the same volume in the same wall-clock second get
      different PIDs, cannot collide.
    - WWID is preserved across Pure-side rename; the plugin's
      WWID tracking JSON keys on WWID (not name), so no tracking
      update is required.
    - opt-out: caller can pass `tombstone => 0` to skip rename
      entirely; the regex check already prevents accidental
      double-tombstoning for callers that don't.

  * Rollback on destroy failure: if rename succeeds but the
    subsequent destroy fails (e.g., volume has unexpected
    protection group attachment, pod in degraded state, transient
    API error), volume_delete now tries to rename the volume BACK
    to its original name before propagating the destroy error.
    This restores pre-call state so the operator's retry runs
    naturally — without the rollback, the next free_image attempt
    would look up the original name, fail with "not found"
    (volume now under tombstone name), and require manual array-
    side cleanup. Rollback is best-effort: if it ALSO fails (rare,
    implies array-wide issue), we log enough detail (tombstone
    name) for manual cleanup from Pure UI.

  * Note on snapshot rollback (PVE revert to snapshot): unrelated
    to this change. PVE snapshot rollback uses volume_overwrite
    (POST /volumes?names=X&overwrite=true) which mutates an
    existing volume's contents in-place; no volume is destroyed,
    so the tombstone path is not entered.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Wed, 13 May 2026 18:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.14-1) stable; urgency=high

  * [HIGH] VM snapshot WITH memory on a multipath-degraded host (e.g.
    4-portal Pure with 2 paths broken) wedged pvedaemon / pvestatd
    and eventually made the entire PVE management plane unresponsive
    ("?" everywhere in the web UI). CT snapshot and VM snapshot
    WITHOUT memory worked fine. Root cause: VM-with-memory snapshot
    creates a VMSTATE volume on the array, which then has to be
    activated host-side (iSCSI session rescan + multipath wait). The
    rescan path used `iscsiadm -m session --rescan`, which in one
    invocation tries to rescan every active session including the
    dead ones. The dead sessions queue SCSI commands that wait for
    kernel-level timeouts (30s+ per dead path), the iscsiadm
    parent gets killed at our wrapper timeout, leaving D-state
    children behind. Every pvestatd poll (10s) re-fired the same
    rescan, stacking more D-state children until the management
    plane died. Reproducer: 4-LIF Pure with 2 broken paths, take a
    VM snapshot with memory; node degrades over time, often within
    one snapshot operation. Fixed by rewriting rescan_sessions to
    enumerate sessions via /sys/class/iscsi_session/ (kernel-
    maintained, immune to iscsiadm hangs), check each session's
    `state` attribute, and only issue per-session rescans
    (`iscsiadm -m session -r <sid> --rescan`) on sessions in
    LOGGED_IN state, each bounded by a 10s timeout. Sessions in
    FREE / REOPEN / FAILED / etc. states are skipped silently;
    worst-case orphan child count drops from "one per dead session
    per poll forever" to "one per stuck LOGGED_IN session per call,
    bounded". Reported by @pulipulichen (#5) with the critical
    "CT works, VM-with-memory doesn't" reproducer that pinpointed
    VMSTATE volume activation as the trigger.

  * [MEDIUM] PVE Web UI disk list silently empty when the storage ID
    contains a `.` (dot) character. encode_volume_name strips dots
    via sanitize_for_pure when writing volumes to the array, but
    list_images and six sibling pattern-building sites did only
    `$storeid =~ s/-/_/g` — leaving the dot in the filter pattern.
    Filter pattern `pve-pure_plugin_5.111_pvepod2-*` therefore never
    matched volumes actually stored as `pve-pure_plugin_5111_pvepod2-...`,
    so list_images returned empty and PVE displayed no disks. Field
    reproducer: storage ID `pure-plugin-5.111-pvepod2` showed empty
    disk list; renaming to `pure-plugin-5-pvepod2` (no dot) made it
    work. Fixed by adding a `storeid_to_pure_prefix($storeid)` helper
    in Naming.pm that does the full sanitize+underscore transform
    that encode_volume_name uses, and replacing all 7 inline
    duplications in PureStoragePlugin.pm with calls to it. The 4
    inline duplications in Naming.pm itself (encode_config_volume_name,
    pve_volname_to_pure for cloudinit/vmstate) also collapsed onto
    the same helper to keep the transform single-source. Reported
    by @pulipulichen (#6).

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Wed, 13 May 2026 12:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.13-1) stable; urgency=high

  * [HIGH] Snapshot rollback silently no-op on REST API 2.x. The
    volume_overwrite sub used PATCH /volumes with a `source` body
    field, but per the FA 2.x OpenAPI spec PATCH /volumes is the
    rename/destroy/modify endpoint and does not accept `source`.
    Pure responded with "No attribute specified." while the API
    returned 200 with empty body, so the PVE task appeared to
    succeed even though the volume contents were never restored.
    Operators saw post-snapshot data still present after rollback.
    Fixed by switching to POST /volumes?names=X&overwrite=true with
    `source` in body — the same endpoint volume_clone already uses,
    with the spec-defined `overwrite=true` query parameter for the
    object-copy case. add_to_protection_group_names and
    with_default_protection are deliberately omitted on this path
    because the spec forbids them when overwrite=true. Reported
    independently by @tgdfama1 (issue #1) and @pulipulichen (#2)
    with a reproducer that creates a snapshot, modifies the volume,
    rolls back from PVE UI, and observes post-snapshot files still
    present.

  * [MEDIUM] Pod-backed storage reported 100% used in PVE the moment
    a thin volume of the quota's size was provisioned, even with
    zero host writes, while the Pure UI correctly showed the pod
    as nearly empty. Root cause: get_managed_capacity preferred
    `total_provisioned` (sum of volume sizes) over `virtual` (host-
    visible logical writes) in the // fallback chain, on the theory
    that pod quotas enforce against provisioned capacity. The
    enforcement theory is correct but the resulting status display
    visibly diverged from Pure's own UI and made operators think
    the storage was full. Switched the fallback chain to prefer
    `virtual` (matching Pure UI) → `total_physical` → `total_used`
    → `total_provisioned`. If the array genuinely runs into the
    quota at allocate time it returns a clear quota error to
    volume_create which translate_pure_error already surfaces, so
    status() does not need to pre-pessimise the cap. Reported by
    @pulipulichen (#3).

  * CI: new manually-triggered GitHub Actions workflow at
    .github/workflows/build-deb.yml that runs `make test` and
    `dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b` on an ubuntu-24.04 runner and
    uploads the produced .deb as an artifact. Lets release
    engineers and contributors grab a packaged build directly from
    the GitHub Actions UI without setting up a Debian dev box.
    Triggered via workflow_dispatch only (no auto-push). Adapted
    from @pulipulichen's contribution in issue #4.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 11 May 2026 12:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.12-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [MEDIUM] pod_get_quota_limit() simplified: now reads ONLY the
    Pod object's own quota_limit field. v1.1.10 also walked the
    /policies/quota and /policies/quota/rules endpoints in an
    attempt to honour quota policies attached to a pod (the path
    the Pure GUI's Storage > Policies builds), but field experience
    shows that mechanism is the WRONG mechanism for block volume
    pod quotas — the entire /policies/* family in Pure (autodir,
    nfs, smb, quota, snapshot) is FlashArray Files / managed-
    directory only. Reading a file-system quota policy and
    surfacing it as a block-level cap to PVE was misleading, even
    though the API technically returned a value. v1.1.10's
    "with_default_protection=false" mitigation in v1.1.11 also did
    NOT fix the field-reported "Pod contains file systems or
    policies." rejection because Pure refuses block volume create
    for any pod that has a Storage > Policies policy attached,
    regardless of default-protection handling. The cure is on the
    Pure side: destroy the file-services policy and set
    Pod.quota_limit instead. The plugin keeps reading
    Pod.quota_limit, which IS the only block-level pod quota
    mechanism Pure exposes (CLI: purepod --quota-limit; REST:
    PATCH /pods body quota_limit; GUI: Purity 6.6+ Edit Pod).

  * Documentation: README and README_zh-TW Option A "Pod with quota"
    sections now spell out the three correct paths (CLI, REST, GUI
    6.6+) for setting Pod.quota_limit, and warn explicitly that
    Storage > Policies must NOT be used for pod block quota — with
    the exact error message and the destroy + re-set recipe so
    operators can self-recover.

  * v1.1.11's with_default_protection=false on volume_create and
    volume_clone is preserved. It does not fix the case it was
    written for, but it remains a correct defensive change: the
    plugin manages its own snapshot policy via volume_snapshot /
    volume_overwrite and never relied on Pure's container default
    protection. No regression risk.

