jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.10-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [MEDIUM] Pod quota capacity reporting was broken: when the storage
    was configured with --pure-pod, status() always reported the FULL
    FlashArray capacity instead of the pod's quota. Pure FlashArray
    API 2.x exposes pod quotas through TWO mechanisms and the old
    code missed both common variants:
    (a) The Pod object itself has a quota_limit field set via
        purepod create --quota-limit / setattr --quota-limit (Purity
        6.4.4+ CLI). The old code read this — but it remains 0 when
        the cap is set via mechanism (b) below.
    (b) Newer Purity also lets the operator create a Policy of
        policy_type=quota that references the pod via the policy's
        own 'pod' field, with one or more Rules in
        /policies/quota/rules carrying the actual quota_limit. The
        Storage > Policies GUI builds quotas this way, and it does
        NOT propagate the cap back into the Pod's own quota_limit.
        So reading the Pod object alone always saw 0.
    Old code therefore always saw quota=0 and fell through to
    array_space(). Field reproducer: Purity//FA 6.5.9 with a quota
    policy 'pvepodquota' (2 TB rule) on pod 'pvepod' showed up in
    PVE as the array's full multi-TB capacity. Fixed by walking both
    paths: read the Pod's own quota_limit, then GET /policies/quota
    filtered by pod.name to find policy-based caps, then GET
    /policies/quota/rules to pull the rule limits. Take the smallest
    positive value across all sources — most-restrictive cap matches
    what the array itself enforces. Edge cases handled: multiple
    rules/policies per pod (min); enabled=false / destroyed
    policies (skipped); enforced=false soft rules (still counted, so
    PVE allocation respects user intent); endpoint 404 / 403 / 400
    on older Purity (no-filter fallback then warn + array fallback);
    pod names with quote/backslash chars (skipped); API 1.x (skipped).
    All failure paths fall back to array capacity with a warning,
    never croak — status() polling must not break on quota lookup.
    Earlier draft of this fix used /policies/quota/members which is
    WRONG for pods (per Pure API 2.26 spec, that membership table
    binds policies to managed directories only). Verified against the
    FA 2.26 OpenAPI spec at code.purestorage.com.

  * Pod 'used' capacity now derived from total_provisioned (the metric
    Pure quotas actually count against, per the FA 2.26 Pod space
    schema) instead of total_used (post-reduction physical bytes).
    Falls back to virtual / total_used / total_physical when
    total_provisioned is absent on older Purity. Without this change,
    a freshly-provisioned 2 T volume in a 2 T pod showed PVE 0% used
    while the pod was already 100% full from the array's perspective;
    the next allocation would have been rejected by the array even
    though PVE thought there was 2 T free.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 08 May 2026 00:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.9-1) stable; urgency=high

  * [HIGH] activate_storage() now TCP-probes every iSCSI portal returned
    by iscsi_get_ports() before invoking iscsiadm discovery/login, and
    skips portals that do not accept a TCP connection within
    pure-portal-probe-timeout seconds (default 2s). Without this guard,
    each unreachable portal stalls activate_storage() for 30s
    (discovery) plus up to 60s (login); a Pure with 4 iSCSI LIFs and
    asymmetric cabling that only reaches one controller therefore wedged
    'pvesm add' and every subsequent pvestatd poll, leaving the web UI
    Status panel in a permanent loading state and starving other
    storage. Reproducer in field: 4-LIF Pure + 2-node PVE where only
    one node could reach 2 of the 4 LIFs; 'pvesm add purestorage'
    returned only after the 2 unreachable LIFs each timed out at line
    PureStoragePlugin.pm:1352 (discover_targets). The same probe is
    applied to the secondary login site in alloc_image() that
    re-establishes sessions for state/cloudinit volumes. If no portal
    is reachable, activate_storage() now fails fast with an actionable
    message pointing at network/zoning checks and the --nodes option.

  * New storage option pure-portal-probe-timeout (integer, 0..30,
    default 2). Set to 0 to disable the pre-check (1.1.8 behaviour) on
    networks where TCP setup latency exceeds 30s but iSCSI itself
    converges; raise on high-latency or congested storage networks.

