jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.8-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * [MEDIUM] _cleanup_orphaned_devices() now untracks WWIDs only after
    verifying the local multipath device is gone. Previously, the
    function unconditionally called _untrack_wwid() after
    cleanup_lun_devices(), even when cleanup failed. Because Phase 1
    cannot re-import a WWID whose volume has been deleted from the
    array, a single transient cleanup failure (kpartx holder,
    multipathd glitch, dmsetup busy) silently leaked a stale device
    that no future status() poll could find. The fix mirrors the
    conditional-untrack pattern free_image() has used since 1.1.x:
    if get_multipath_device($wwid) still returns a path after cleanup,
    keep the WWID tracked so the next pass retries; only untrack when
    verifiably gone.

  * [LOW] glob("/dev/disk/by-id/...") calls now wrapped in a 5-second
    alarm timeout in Multipath::get_device_by_wwid,
    ISCSI::wait_for_device, and ISCSI::get_device_by_serial. The -b
    stat that follows the glob in get_device_by_wwid resolves the
    symlink to /dev/sd* or /dev/dm-*; on a multipath device with all
    paths down and queue_if_no_path still active, this stat hits the
    same kernel block-layer wait that blocks vgs and lvs. Pattern
    matches existing _run_cmd / sysfs_read_with_timeout style.

  * Sibling-pattern audit driven by the author's related
    jt-pve-storage-netapp plugin v0.2.9 fixes. Three of the NetApp
    issues did not have a counterpart here: Pure uses
    volume names directly as identifiers (no lookup-after-create
    eventual consistency window); alloc_image was already a bounded
    retry loop matching clone_image; multipath -F is already a
    forbidden pattern.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Sat, 26 Apr 2026 12:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.7-1) stable; urgency=high

  * [CRITICAL] is_device_in_use() treated ALL holders as "device in
    use", including bare kpartx partition devices that the Linux kernel
    creates automatically on any multipath LUN with a GPT/MBR partition
    table (i.e. every single VM with an OS installed). This made it
    impossible to delete any VM disk on Pure storage when the host
    kernel had auto-scanned the LUN content. The fix distinguishes
    bare kpartx partitions from real holders (LVM, dm-crypt, dm-raid,
    bcache). A partition is SAFE to ignore if: its dm-name matches a
    known kpartx pattern (<wwid>-part1, <wwid>p1, <wwid>1, mpath0-part1,
    sdf1), it has NO sub-holders, it is NOT mounted (/proc/mounts),
    and it is NOT used as swap (/proc/swaps). If ANY holder is NOT a
    partition, or any partition has sub-holders/mount/swap, deletion is
    still blocked (data-loss protection preserved).

  * [HIGH] cleanup_lun_devices() now runs `kpartx -d <device>` before
    attempting to remove the multipath map. Without this, partition
    holder devices prevent `multipathd remove map` and `multipath -f`
    from succeeding, because partition dm devices are holders of the
    multipath map.

  * [MEDIUM] get_device_usage_details() no longer misparses kpartx
    partition dm-names as LVM VG names. The dm-name
    `3624a9370...-part1` was being parsed as VG `3624a9370...` LV
    `part1`. Partition patterns are now checked first and excluded from
    VG name parsing.

  * [LOW] Orphan warning cooldown: Phase 3 untracked-device warnings
    in _cleanup_orphaned_devices now use a per-WWID flag file in
    /var/run/pve-storage-purestorage/ (tmpfs, cleared on reboot) to
    limit warnings to once per hour per WWID. Previously, pvestatd's
    10-second status() polling would fire the same warning every 10
    seconds, flooding the journal with identical messages for any
    untracked Pure multipath device.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 11 Apr 2026 12:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.6-1) stable; urgency=high

  * [CRITICAL] postinst now reloads ALL three PVE services that load
    storage plugin code: pvedaemon, pvestatd, pveproxy. Previous
    versions did not reload ANY of them, meaning old bug-containing
    code (e.g. the pre-1.1.5 rescan_scsi_hosts that writes to all
    SCSI hosts) stayed in memory indefinitely until manual service
    restart. pvestatd is particularly dangerous: it polls status()
    every 10 seconds, and if the old code triggers D-state children
    (e.g. on HPE smartpqi), the node accumulates unkillable D-state
    processes until a hardware watchdog or manual reboot intervenes.
    Changed from `systemctl restart` to `systemctl reload` (SIGHUP)
    to avoid the stop-phase hang: if the old code created D-state
    children, the stop phase blocks waiting for unkillable processes.
    reload sends SIGHUP which makes PVE::Daemon re-exec() itself
    with new code, bypassing the stop phase entirely.

  * [HIGH] postinst now checks /etc/lvm/lvm.conf for `global_filter`
    and warns if absent. On PVE nodes upgraded from 7/8 to 9, the old
    lvm.conf lacks the filter that excludes device-mapper and
    multipath devices from LVM scanning. Without it, the host LVM
    auto-activates VGs found inside guest VM disks (which are raw
    LUNs visible as multipath devices), creating holder dm devices
    on top of the multipath device. These holders cause
    is_device_in_use() to correctly block free_image from deleting
    the volume. The warning includes the recommended filter setting
    and the exact commands to deactivate already-activated guest VGs.

  * [HIGH] free_image() now provides detailed usage information when
    is_device_in_use() blocks deletion. New get_device_usage_details()
    helper in Multipath.pm enumerates holder device names, dm-names,
    detects LVM VG names from dm-name conventions, and explains the
    root cause (host LVM auto-activation on upgraded PVE nodes) with
    exact remediation: `vgchange -an <vg>` to deactivate immediately,
    `global_filter` setting for long-term fix.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 10 Apr 2026 14:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.5-1) stable; urgency=high

  * [CRITICAL] rescan_scsi_hosts() iterated every entry in
    /sys/class/scsi_host/, including non-iSCSI hosts (HPE Smart Array
    P408i-a and similar HBAs). Writing "- - -" to those scan files
    triggered driver-side full target rescans which entered D-state for
    600+ seconds inside the smartpqi / megaraid_sas / mpt3sas drivers.
    sysfs_write_with_timeout() protects the parent process but does
    NOT protect the kernel: D-state children cannot be reaped by
    SIGKILL and they hold kernel scan locks until the driver finishes,
    causing cascading config-lock timeouts on every subsequent VM
    operation, and pvedaemon restart hangs requiring force-reboot. The
    bug was latent in every release prior to 1.1.5 and would have
    surfaced as soon as a customer deployed on HPE ProLiant, Dell PERC,
    Lenovo ThinkSystem, or any server with a SAS HBA / hardware RAID
    controller. Fixed by sourcing the host list from
    /sys/class/iscsi_host/ instead of /sys/class/scsi_host/. The
    scsi_transport_iscsi layer registers every iSCSI host there via
    iscsi_host_alloc(), regardless of underlying driver (iscsi_tcp,
    iser, bnx2i, qla4xxx, qedi, be2iscsi, cxgb3i, cxgb4i, ...). Non-
    iSCSI drivers categorically never register there, so iterating
    that class is both exhaustive and safe. Verified on a real host
    with mixed scsi_host (host0-3 non-iSCSI + host4-7 iSCSI): strace
    confirms writes only happen on host4-7 after the fix.

  * [HIGH] FC.pm rescan_fc_hosts() used bare open() to write
    /sys/class/fc_host/<host>/issue_lip and /sys/class/scsi_host/<host>/scan,
    which can hang the parent process if the FC HBA is wedged. The
    SCSI scan loop already filtered to FC hosts only (via
    get_fc_hosts() — no Bug 1 risk there), but the bare open() means
    the parent worker stalls instead of moving on after a timeout.
    Fixed by routing both writes through sysfs_write_with_timeout(),
    matching the protection already applied in Multipath.pm.

  * [LOW operator UX] Add translate_pure_error() helper in API.pm and
    apply it to alloc_image / clone_image / volume_snapshot die sites.
    Pure FlashArray's raw API errors tell operators WHAT happened
    (e.g. "Maximum number of volumes reached") but not WHY (what's
    the cap?) or HOW to recover (delete things, wait for eradication,
    raise the limit). The translator pattern-matches Pure's known
    limit errors (volume count, snapshot count, host connection
    count, protection group count, capacity exhaustion, API rate
    limit) and prepends a human summary while preserving the
    original error. Unknown errors pass through unchanged.

  * Six more bugs found by an internal deep audit after 1.1.3, applying
    the "sibling pattern" rule (every bug fix triggers a codebase-wide
    search for the same anti-pattern) to every cleanup path, /sys/block
    access, and API version-divergence point.

  * [HIGH] volume_get_connections() did not normalise the API 1.x
    response shape. Pure REST 1.x returns
    `[{ host => "h1", lun => 1, name => "myvol" }, ...]` where the
    `name` field is the VOLUME name, not the host name. The 2.x branch
    was already normalised to `{ name => "<host>" }`. Every caller
    (free_image, _disconnect_from_all_hosts, _backup_vm_config,
    _cleanup_orphaned_temp_clones, _cleanup_temp_snap_clone,
    alloc_image orphan-cleanup) iterated `$conn->{name}`, which on
    1.x returned the VOLUME name. The subsequent
    `volume_disconnect_host($vol, $conn->{name})` therefore passed the
    volume name as the host argument, which silently fails inside an
    eval. **Result on API 1.x: every disconnect call was a no-op,
    leaving orphaned host connections forever, and every volume_delete
    cleanup hit the Bug E ghost-LUN failure mode.** Fix: normalise the
    API 1.x branch in volume_get_connections() to the same
    `[{ name => "<host>" }]` shape, with fallback to `host_name` and
    `name` fields.

  * [HIGH] path() temp clone connect-failure had two bugs in one
    sequence: (a) Bug E pattern — `volume_delete($temp)` called
    without disconnect first, (b) `$@` clobber — the inner cleanup
    eval reset `$@` so the subsequent `die "...$@"` showed the
    cleanup error instead of the original connect error. Fixed both:
    save `$connect_err = $@` first, then call
    `_disconnect_from_all_hosts` before `volume_delete`, then die with
    the saved error.

  * [HIGH] _backup_vm_config() connect-failure had the same Bug E
    pattern: volume_connect_host fails -> volume_delete without
    disconnect -> orphaned host connection on the array. Fixed by
    calling _disconnect_from_all_hosts before volume_delete in both
    the connect-fail branch and the "Cannot get WWID" branch.

  * [MEDIUM] clone_image() was missing disk-id collision retry —
    same TOCTOU window as alloc_image had before 1.1.0. Two
    concurrent `qm clone` invocations on the same source VM could
    both pick the same disk id from _find_free_diskid and one would
    fail with "already exists". Fixed with a 5-attempt retry loop
    around the volume_clone call: on collision, refetch the next
    free id and retry.

  * [LOW] rescan_scsi_device() used basename() instead of
    _resolve_block_device_name(). Current callers always pass /dev/sdX
    so the bug is latent, but as an exported helper a future caller
    passing /dev/mapper/<wwid> would silently fail. Fixed
    defensively for consistency with the rest of the Multipath module
    after the 1.1.2 Bug C/D fixes.

  * [LOW] _backup_vm_config() used bare system() for mkfs.ext4 / mount
    / umount. The 1MB volume is freshly allocated so the device is
    healthy in normal operation, but a wedged multipath device would
    cause mount to enter D state. Replaced all four with
    PVE::Tools::run_command(..., timeout => 30) and added an explicit
    sync before umount. The error-path umount also gets a 30s timeout.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 09 Apr 2026 20:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.3-1) stable; urgency=high

  * Three more bugs found by a proactive sibling-pattern audit after
    the 1.1.2 release. All three follow the rule "when you fix a bug
    pattern, the same pattern often exists elsewhere you haven't
    checked." Pure plugin had every one of them.

  * [HIGH] Bug E: alloc_image() and clone_image() cleanup-on-failure
    paths previously called volume_delete() without first disconnecting
    the volume from the cluster hosts that _connect_to_all_hosts() may
    have already partially connected to. _connect_to_all_hosts iterates
    every cluster host in per-node mode; if it succeeds on hosts 1..K
    and fails on K+1, the volume is still mapped to K hosts when the
    cleanup runs. Pure (unlike ONTAP) will physically destroy a still-
    connected volume, but the orphaned host connection records cause
    iSCSI rescan on other cluster nodes to discover ghost LUNs that
    become stale multipath devices. Combined with `no_path_retry queue`
    in /etc/multipath.conf defaults — same root cause as the production
    hang incident that drove v1.1.0. Fixed by adding a
    _disconnect_from_all_hosts() helper that queries the array for the
    current connection list and disconnects each, and calling it BEFORE
    volume_delete in every cleanup path. The same fix is applied to
    four sites: alloc_image() main connect-fail cleanup, alloc_image()
    state/cloudinit "Cannot get WWID" cleanup, alloc_image() state/
    cloudinit "device did not appear" cleanup, and clone_image()
    connect-fail cleanup.

  * [LOW] Bug F: volume_snapshot() now flushes host-side dirty buffers
    before calling snapshot_create on the array, mirroring what
    volume_snapshot_rollback() already did before the rollback. For
    running VMs the qemu freeze handles consistency at the filesystem
    layer, but for offline volumes or external script callers (e.g.
    backup tools writing directly to a stopped-VM volume) the dirty
    page cache could be missing from the snapshot, producing a
    filesystem-inconsistent capture. Guarded by is_device_in_use() so
    we don't block on a busy live migration.

  * [LOW] Bug G + dead export audit: removed four exported but
    zero-caller functions from Multipath.pm — multipath_add,
    multipath_remove, get_multipath_wwid, get_scsi_devices_by_serial.
    get_multipath_wwid had a latent /dev/mapper symlink bug like the
    one fixed in is_device_in_use in 1.1.2; rather than fix dead code
    (and risk a future contributor seeing it in @EXPORT_OK and calling
    it), the function is removed entirely. The other three were also
    unused.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 09 Apr 2026 19:30:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.2-1) stable; urgency=high

  * CRITICAL: four bugs ported from jt-pve-storage-netapp v0.2.x post-release
    forensics. The Pure plugin had the same broken patterns as the NetApp
    plugin discovered after a real customer resize incident on 2026-04-09.
    All four are CVE-class for production environments.

  * [CRITICAL — DATA LOSS] is_device_in_use() previously called
    basename($device) and looked at /sys/block/<basename>/holders. For a
    multipath device path /dev/mapper/3624a9370... that resolved to
    /sys/block/3624a9370.../holders, which DOES NOT EXIST — the holders
    directory lives under /sys/block/dm-N/. The function therefore
    returned 0 (not in use) for any multipath device, regardless of
    whether an LVM volume group, dm-crypt container, or any other holder
    sat on top of it. free_image() called this check before deleting,
    saw "not in use", and proceeded to destroy the volume — taking the
    customer's LVM data with it. Any production environment that uses
    LVM (or dm-crypt / dm-raid / bcache / ...) on top of Pure-managed
    volumes was at risk of silent data loss. Fixed by adding a
    _resolve_block_device_name() helper that resolves /dev/mapper/<wwid>
    symlinks to the underlying dm-N name before any /sys/block/ access.

  * [HIGH] get_multipath_slaves() had the same broken pattern. It always
    returned an empty list for /dev/mapper/<wwid> paths, which meant
    free_image()'s post-cleanup SCSI slave removal silently skipped every
    device, leaking SCSI residue across operations. Fixed via the same
    _resolve_block_device_name() helper.

  * [HIGH] volume_resize() called rescan_scsi_hosts() (host scan, used
    to discover NEW devices) instead of per-device rescan (used to
    re-read attributes of EXISTING devices). After a Pure-side resize the
    array showed the new size, but the multipath device kept reporting
    the old size, and QEMU's block_resize then failed with "Cannot grow
    device files" on a running VM. Fixed to do per-slave
    `echo 1 > /sys/block/sdX/device/rescan` followed by
    `multipathd resize map <name>` (a new helper) to refresh the size of
    the device-mapper layer above.

  * [HIGH] volume_snapshot_rollback() had the same wrong rescan as Bug A,
    plus a second issue: even after the underlying SCSI paths were
    refreshed, the kernel buffer cache could still hold pages from the
    post-snapshot content. Subsequent reads from the rolled-back volume
    could return stale data. Fixed to (1) per-slave rescan, (2)
    multipath_resize_map, AND (3) `blockdev --flushbufs <device>` to
    invalidate the kernel buffer cache.

  * Add multipath_resize_map() helper in Multipath.pm for the resize/
    rollback fixes. Wrapped in @EXPORT_OK for use from the main plugin.

  * Add _resolve_block_device_name() helper in Multipath.pm. Use it from
    is_device_in_use() and get_multipath_slaves() — every other function
    in the codebase that touches /sys/block/ should also be reviewed
    against this pattern, and a checklist test added in TESTING.md.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 09 Apr 2026 19:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-purestorage (1.1.1-1) stable; urgency=high

  * Multipath / anti-hang follow-ups discovered while reviewing v1.1.0
    against the PVE storage plugin development guide:
    - Pure multipath device block now sets `no_path_retry 30` and
      `fast_io_fail_tmo 5` explicitly. Without these, the device inherited
      the defaults section value, which on many sites is `queue` (the
      NetApp HA recommendation). Combined with a stale device this caused
      sync/blockdev/multipath -f to enter D state — exactly what we were
      trying to prevent.
    - _ensure_multipath_config now writes a version marker into the
      generated /etc/multipath/conf.d/pure-storage.conf and rewrites
      plugin-managed files when the version changes. Files without the
      marker are still left untouched (operator-edited / third-party).
      This means a 1.0.x → 1.1.x upgrade actually picks up the new
      no_path_retry safety setting instead of silently keeping the old
      file forever.
    - Replace bare system('fuser', ...) in is_device_in_use with a
      timeout-bounded _run_cmd. fuser opens the device path; on a wedged
      multipath device with queue_if_no_path it can itself enter D state.
    - Replace bare system('sync')/system('blockdev', ...) in volume_resize
      with timeout-bounded PVE::Tools::run_command.
    - Add _udev_refresh() helper that calls `udevadm trigger` and
      `udevadm settle` via PVE::Tools::run_command with a 10s timeout,
      and replace all bare system('udevadm ...') calls in the plugin and
      the Multipath module.

